{"documentId":"tos.work.sextus-empiricus.outlines-of-pyrrhonism","versionId":"retained-outlines-en-forage2026-ai","revision":"5796826c1e9b123b1253409ad3056e735c353f4f4bf94b1bdf84be7251587ff7","units":[{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-41a3ac395bc74369914de3af0717e235","ordinal":481,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 2 · §2.240","text":"\n\nknowing which affections are primarily and directly relevant and which are their symptoms, will say that the argument applies not to the symptoms (and in fact, when cold water is poured on, the fever happens to get worse) but to the directly relevant affections. The constriction is the directly relevant affection, and it calls not for condensing but for the relaxing type of treatment. The heat that follows on it is not primarily relevant, and so neither is what seems suited to it."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-f078b59cba0543d3be3ae34b28ffdc39","ordinal":482,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 2 · §2.241","text":"\n\nSo with the sophisms whose solution is usefully required, the dialectician will have nothing to say. Instead he puts to us arguments like these: “If it is not the case both that you have beautiful horns and that you have horns, you have horns; but it is not the case both that you have beautiful horns and that you have horns;"},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-9587aca6293748bb93b8d4f6df4bd7c2","ordinal":483,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 2 · §2.242","text":"\n\ntherefore you have horns.” “If something moves, it moves either in the place where it is or in the place where it is not. But not in the place where it is (for it is at rest there), nor in the place where it is not (for how could anything act in a place where it is not at all?);"},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-ac6b1af98198412ea780204601843757","ordinal":484,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 2 · §2.243","text":"\n\ntherefore nothing moves.” “Either what is comes to be or what is not. What is does not come to be (for it is); nor does what is not (for what comes to be undergoes something, but what is not undergoes nothing); therefore nothing comes to be.”"},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-b8fec0d93978466ca6b7d0d1dd65aafa","ordinal":485,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 2 · §2.244","text":"\n\n“Snow is frozen water; but water is black; therefore snow is black.” Having collected rubbish of this sort, he knits his brows, brings out dialectic, and very solemnly tries to establish for us by syllogistic proofs that something comes to be, that something moves, that snow is white, and that we do not have horns. Yet perhaps it would be enough to set clarity against them, to shatter their confident position by the equipollent counter-testimony of appearances. That is why a ⟨certain⟩ philosopher, when an argument against motion was put to him, walked about in silence. And people in everyday life set out on journeys by land and sea, build ships and houses, and have children, paying no attention to the arguments against motion and coming-to-be.\n〔1〕 The philosopher is traditionally Diogenes the Cynic, answering arguments against motion like those of Zeno of Elea."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-810db278acd14fa38e2cc8e95f7f811f","ordinal":486,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 2 · §2.245","text":"\n\nThere is also a charming anecdote told of Herophilus the doctor. He was a contemporary of Diodorus, who indulged in dialectic in poor taste and used to go through sophistical arguments against motion, among many other things. Once Diodorus dislocated his shoulder and came to Herophilus to be treated. Herophilus teased him, saying: “Your shoulder slipped out either in the place where it was or in the place where it was not; but neither in the place where it was nor in the place where it was not; therefore it has not slipped out.” And the sophist begged him to drop arguments like that and give him the appropriate medical treatment."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-e345cfaa53184731b99616e66085ff6b","ordinal":487,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 2 · §2.246","text":"\n\nFor it is enough, I think, to live by experience and without beliefs, in accordance with common observations and preconceptions, suspending judgment about what is said out of dogmatic over-subtlety and, above all, about what lies outside the needs of life. So dialectic could not solve the sophisms whose solution would be useful, and the solution of those sophisms one might perhaps grant it can solve is useless. If so, dialectic is useless when it comes to solving sophisms."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-1d3a172bcda746b3b9b607e772db053a","ordinal":488,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 2 · §2.247","text":"\n\nStarting from the very things the dialecticians say, one could also briefly point out, as follows, that their technical treatment of sophisms is superfluous. The dialecticians say that they took up the art of dialectic not simply in order to know what follows from what, but chiefly in order to know how to judge truths and falsehoods by means of demonstrative arguments. At any rate, they say that dialectic is the science of what is true, what is false, and what is neither."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-f0c6f42224cb428f82e262406f3ec6c7","ordinal":489,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 2 · §2.248","text":"\n\nNow, they themselves say that a true argument is one that concludes a true conclusion by means of true premises. So as soon as an argument with a false conclusion is put to us, we will know that it is false and will not assent to it. For the argument itself must either not be conclusive or not have true premises."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-6e3f1e7fd3324428b316a3e0136381d7","ordinal":490,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 2 · §2.249","text":"\n\nThis is clear from the following. Either the false conclusion of the argument follows from the conjunction of its premises or it does not. If it does not follow, the argument will not even be conclusive; for they say an argument is conclusive when its conclusion follows from the conjunction of its premises. If it does follow, then the conjunction of the premises must also be false, by their own technical rules. For they say that what is false follows from what is false, but never from what is true."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-4bb50be4b28d45e79ae2d0507d92e3e8","ordinal":491,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 2 · §2.250","text":"\n\nAnd it is clear from what was said earlier that an argument that is not conclusive, or not true, is not demonstrative either, according to them. So when an argument with a false conclusion is put to us, we know at once, from the fact that its conclusion is false, that the argument is not true or not conclusive. Then we will not assent to it, even if we do not know where its deceptiveness lies. We do not assent to the tricks of conjurors as true, but know that they are deceiving us, even if we do not know how they deceive us. In the same way we are not persuaded by arguments that are false but seem persuasive, even if we do not know how they mislead."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-e141f22ed93f42e29cf99fea288eebc0","ordinal":492,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 2 · §2.251","text":"\n\nOr, since they say that a sophism leads not only to something false but also to other absurdities, the argument must be put more generally, as follows. The argument propounded leads us either to something unacceptable or to something we ought to accept. If the latter, we will assent to it without absurdity. But if it leads to something unacceptable, it is not we who must rashly assent to the absurdity because of its plausibility. Rather, they must abandon the argument that compels assent to absurdities—if, that is, they have chosen not to babble like children but to seek the truth, as they promise."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-befdce6dd2dc4cac914db4dd9700e51a","ordinal":493,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 2 · §2.252","text":"\n\nSuppose there were a road leading to a cliff. We would not throw ourselves over the cliff because there is a road leading to it; we would turn off the road because of the cliff. In the same way, if there is an argument leading us to something admittedly absurd, we will not assent to the absurdity because of the argument; we will abandon the argument because of the absurdity."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-8893221851c04ab5804fd04dc67a45d1","ordinal":494,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 2 · §2.253","text":"\n\nSo when an argument is put to us in this way, we will suspend judgment on each premise, and then, when the whole argument has been put, we will add what seems right ⟨to us⟩. Chrysippus and the dogmatists of his school say that, when the sorites is being put, they must stop and suspend judgment as the argument proceeds, so as not to fall into absurdity. If so, it would surely be much more fitting for us, as skeptics who suspect absurdity, not to rush ahead as the premises are put, but to suspend judgment on each one until the whole argument has been put.\n〔1〕 The sorites (“heap”) asks, one grain at a time, when a heap begins; Chrysippus advised falling silent before the doubtful steps."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-d2d5d36645da4b90a0aec0f738156cbf","ordinal":495,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 2 · §2.254","text":"\n\nAnd we, starting without beliefs from the observation of everyday life, avoid deceptive arguments in this way. The dogmatists, though, will be unable to tell a sophism apart from an argument that seems properly put, if they must judge dogmatically that the form of the argument is conclusive and that the premises are true, or that this is not so."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-d6d45b27d8564159927df6cc7f6a2542","ordinal":496,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 2 · §2.255","text":"\n\nFor we pointed out earlier that they can neither apprehend conclusive arguments nor judge that anything is true, since they have no agreed criterion or proof; we pointed this out from what they themselves say. So, as far as these considerations go, the much-vaunted technical treatment of sophisms among the dialecticians is superfluous."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-805a48650cdc4b96bb353214b58ffaed","ordinal":497,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 2 · §2.256","text":"\n\nWe say similar things about distinguishing ambiguities. An ambiguity is an expression that signifies two or more things, and expressions signify by convention. So the ambiguities that it is useful to resolve, namely those that arise within some field of expertise, will be resolved by people trained in each art. For they themselves are experienced in the conventional use they have made of names for the things signified. But the dialectician will not resolve them at all."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-d80510a732fd4729ab7215eedf59e5f3","ordinal":498,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 2 · §2.257","text":"\n\nTake, for instance, this ambiguity: “In the abatements a varied diet and wine should be approved.” And in everyday life we see even slave boys distinguishing ambiguities when distinguishing them seems useful to them. If someone who had slaves with the same name told a boy to call Manes, say, for him (let that be the name the slaves share), the boy will ask, “Which one?” And if someone who had several different wines said to the boy, “Pour me some of the wine to drink,” the boy will likewise ask, “Which?”"},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-fb3e4e6453be48a5838badfb0a030a16","ordinal":499,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 2 · §2.258","text":"\n\nSo in each field it is experience of what is useful that brings in the distinguishing. But some ambiguities do not arise in any field of everyday experience. They lie in dogmatic opinions and are perhaps useless for living without beliefs. The dialectician, who is peculiarly concerned with these, will be forced to suspend judgment about them too, following the skeptical methods, insofar as they are bound up with things that are non-evident and inapprehensible, or perhaps even unreal."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-8df8a7c983e34daeb838f0f6f1fc8c97","ordinal":500,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 2 · §2.259","text":"\n\nBut we will discuss these matters again later. And if some dogmatist tries to argue against any of this, he will strengthen the skeptical argument. By the arguments on both sides and the undecidable disagreement, he will himself confirm suspension of judgment about the matters under investigation. Having said this much about ambiguities too, we bring the second book of the Outlines to a close here."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-30fc682ba26b448a8e670159de7eded2","ordinal":501,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 3 · §3.1","text":"\n\nSo much, ⟨then⟩, may suffice as an account, in outline, of the logical part of what they call philosophy.\nGoing through its physical part in the same manner of writing, we will not argue against each of their statements one by one. Instead we will try to unsettle the more general points, and the rest are canceled along with these. Let us begin with the account of principles.\nMost thinkers agree that some principles are material and others active. So we will begin our account with the active ones, since they say these are also more important than the material ones."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-1cfab95a64cd4be08732ff44387d35a9","ordinal":502,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 3 · §3.2","text":"\n\nSince most have declared that god is the most active cause, let us first consider god. We should say beforehand that, following ordinary life without holding beliefs, we say that there are gods, we revere gods, and we say that they exercise providence. But against the rashness of the dogmatists we say the following. We ought to form a conception of the substance of the things we conceive of, for example whether they are bodies or incorporeal. We ought also to conceive of their shapes, since no one could conceive of a horse without first having learned the shape of a horse. And what is conceived of ought to be conceived of as being somewhere."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-8a7469f99b834ae38355d6b6af650a1c","ordinal":503,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 3 · §3.3","text":"\n\nAmong the dogmatists, some say that god is a body and others that he is incorporeal. Some say he is human in form, others not. Some say he is in a place, others not, and of those who put him in a place, some put him inside the cosmos and others outside it. How, then, shall we be able to form a concept of god, when we have no agreed account of his substance, or of his form, or of the place where he would be? Let them first come to an agreement and concur that god is of such-and-such a kind. Then, once they have sketched him out for us, let them require us to form a concept of god. As long as they disagree undecidably, we have nothing agreed upon from them about what we are to think."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-25660417396c48aaa0077724c8d9ff9f","ordinal":504,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 3 · §3.4","text":"\n\n“But think of something imperishable and blessed,” they say, “and take that to be god.” This is simple-minded. ⟨For⟩ someone who does not know Dion cannot think of the attributes Dion has as belonging to Dion either. In the same way, since we do not know the substance of god, we shall not be able to learn or conceive his attributes either."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-557ec778f237429683f1b66edef03ed1","ordinal":505,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 3 · §3.5","text":"\n\nBesides this, let them tell us what the blessed is. Is it what acts in accordance with excellence and exercises providence over the things ranked beneath it? Or is it what is inactive, neither having trouble itself nor causing trouble for anyone else? They have disagreed undecidably about this too, and so they have made the blessed inconceivable to us, and for that reason god as well."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-9dd88250a4ea43049908620ee1ed5bc0","ordinal":506,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 3 · §3.6","text":"\n\nAnd even if god is conceived, it is necessary to suspend judgment about whether he exists or not, as far as the dogmatists are concerned. For it is not evident that god exists. If it struck us of itself, the dogmatists would have agreed about who he is, of what sort he is, and where he is. Instead, the undecidable disagreement has made him seem to us non-evident and in need of proof."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-15cbc774c0004e748be468178ea59934","ordinal":507,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 3 · §3.7","text":"\n\nNow anyone ⟨proving⟩ that god exists proves it either through something evident or through something non-evident. Not through something evident, certainly. Suppose what proves that god exists were evident. What is proved is thought of relative to what proves it, and is therefore apprehended together with it, as we have established. So that god exists would also be evident, since it would be apprehended together with what proves it, which is evident. But it is not evident, as we pointed out; so it is not proved through something evident either. Nor is it proved through something non-evident."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-9ccf7eedd5704e85b5ea97ca9761548a","ordinal":508,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 3 · §3.8","text":"\n\nFor the non-evident thing that is supposed to prove that god exists needs proof itself. If it is said to be proved through something evident, it will no longer be non-evident but evident [that god exists]. So the non-evident thing that proves it is not proved through something evident. Nor through something non-evident: whoever says this will fall into infinite regress, since we shall always ask for a proof of the non-evident thing brought forward to prove the one before. Therefore it cannot be proved from something else that god exists."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-e4a2a989d91d4e46b496e72bed092938","ordinal":509,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 3 · §3.9","text":"\n\nBut if it is neither evident of itself nor proved from something else, whether god exists will be inapprehensible. This too should be said. Whoever says that god exists says either that he exercises providence over the things in the cosmos or that he does not; and if he does, then either over all of them or over some. But if he exercised providence over all things, there would be nothing bad and no vice in the cosmos; yet they say that everything is full of vice. So god will not be said to exercise providence over all things."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-3eff68901c6645e4865e733da0a5db15","ordinal":510,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 3 · §3.10","text":"\n\nAnd if he exercises providence over some, why over these and not over those? For either he both wants to and is able to exercise providence over all things; or he wants to but is not able; or he is able but does not want to; or he neither wants to nor is able. If he both wanted to and was able, he would exercise providence over all things. But he does not exercise providence over all things, for the reasons just given. So it is not the case that he both wants to and is able to exercise providence over all things. And if he wants to but is not able, he is weaker than the cause that makes him unable to exercise providence over the things he does not;"},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-ab6a2d051158453a908cdf62ccf73047","ordinal":511,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 3 · §3.11","text":"\n\nbut it goes against the concept of god for him to be weaker than anything. If he is able to exercise providence over all things but does not want to, he would be thought envious. And if he neither wants to nor is able, he is both envious and weak, which only impious people would say about god. So god does not exercise providence over the things in the cosmos. But if he exercises providence over nothing, and there is no work or effect of his, no one will be able to say how it is apprehended that god exists, since he neither appears of himself nor is apprehended through any effects. For these reasons too, then, whether god exists is inapprehensible."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-0cc4919e029c4812841cd358f9ae0929","ordinal":512,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 3 · §3.12","text":"\n\nFrom this we conclude that those who firmly assert that god exists are perhaps forced into impiety. If they say that he exercises providence over all things, they will be saying that god is the cause of bad things. If they say that he exercises providence over some things, or even over none, they will be forced to say that god is either envious or weak. To say these things is plainly impious."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-49e50185155f4a2ab1fe176bd8213d6b","ordinal":513,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 3 · §3.13","text":"\n\nThe dogmatists, at a loss for a way to argue against us on the substance of the matter, might try to revile us as impious too. To prevent this, we shall raise difficulties about the active cause in more general terms, after first trying to examine the concept of cause. As far as what the dogmatists say goes, no one could even conceive of cause. Besides ⟨giving⟩ conflicting and outlandish concepts of cause, they have also made its existence undiscoverable through their disagreement about it."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-693e6361dda54344833e8226599f02d5","ordinal":514,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 3 · §3.14","text":"\n\nSome say that a cause is a body, others that it is incorporeal. In their view, a cause in the more general sense would seem to be that through whose activity the effect comes about. In this sense the sun, or the sun’s heat, is the cause of the wax’s being melted, or of the melting of the wax. For they have disagreed on this point too: some say that a cause is a cause of appellatives, such as “melting”, others that it is a cause of predicates, such as “being melted”. So, as I said, a cause in the more general sense would be that through whose activity the effect comes about."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-b9a872fe2e674fd8b53681ec36e8e7c2","ordinal":515,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 3 · §3.15","text":"\n\nOf these causes, most hold that some are containing causes, some joint causes, and some auxiliary causes. Containing causes are those whose presence brings the effect’s presence, whose removal removes it, and whose diminution diminishes it (that is how they say the placing of a noose is the cause of strangling). A joint cause is one that contributes a power equal to another joint cause toward the effect’s existing (⟨for⟩ that is how they say each of the oxen drawing a plow is a cause of the plow’s being drawn). An auxiliary cause is one that contributes a slight power, and toward the effect’s existing with ease: as when two people are carrying some weight with difficulty and a third comes along and helps lighten it."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-ea46c8478e7d4eafa846b7137112fafb","ordinal":516,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 3 · §3.16","text":"\n\nSome, however, have also said that present things are causes of future things, as antecedent causes are; for example, prolonged exposure to the sun is a cause of fever. Others have rejected these. Since a cause is relative, and relative to the effect, it cannot precede the effect as its cause. In raising difficulties about causes, we say the following."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-1b8470af35f94ddbb94e6f2a7a923ea0","ordinal":517,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 3 · §3.17","text":"\n\nIt is plausible that there is cause. Without some cause, how could there be growth, diminution, generation, destruction, motion in general, each of the physical and psychic effects, the ordering of the whole cosmos, and everything else? Indeed, even if none of these is real in its nature, we shall say that it is surely because of some cause that they appear to us as what they are not."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-ead6520bd3664b659546730f5362fa77","ordinal":518,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 3 · §3.18","text":"\n\nWhat is more, if there were no cause, anything would come from anything, and at random. Horses, for instance, might be born from mice, and elephants from ants. Egyptian Thebes would get heavy rains and snow at times, and the southern regions would get no rain, if there were not some cause that makes the southern regions stormy and those toward the east dry."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-3dd46b2def8a483cbf24e87060b318b3","ordinal":519,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 3 · §3.19","text":"\n\nAnyone who says that there is no cause refutes himself. If he says that he asserts this simply and without any cause, he will not be credible. But if he asserts it because of some cause, then in wanting to abolish cause he posits it. Let him give us the cause through which there is no cause! For these reasons, then, it is plausible that there is cause;"},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-a81c1502da0545dbb1467f62f4e8155d","ordinal":520,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 3 · §3.20","text":"\n\nbut it is also plausible to say that nothing is a cause of anything. This will be clear once we set out a few arguments out of many, for now, to bring the point to mind. For instance, it is impossible to conceive of a cause before apprehending its effect as its effect. We recognize that it is a cause of the effect only when we apprehend that as an effect."}]}